          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
          .standout
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              \. This is something I thought about in the context of my discussion at
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
          %h1#idea Idea
          %p
            We give a description of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-quantity"} ∞-quantity
            of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbf{B}G_e^{(1)})
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial space
            of infinitesimal neighbourhoods of the identity in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            in the simplicial smooth space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            that is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} Lie ∞-groupoid
            incarnation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
          %p
            The derivation is analogous to and usefully compared with how the deRham algebra of differential forms on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-quantity"} ∞-quantity
            of functions on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+singular+simplicial+complex"} infinitesimal singular simplicial complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi diff
                        %mo •
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\Delta^\bullet_{diff}} \hookrightarrow \Pi(X)
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms+in+synthetic+differential+geometry"} differential forms in synthetic differential geometry
          %p We proceed entirely by using theorems and propositions from the book
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              %em Synthetic Geometry of Manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/SGM-final.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            in particular section 6.8 combined with section 4.3. We effectively show that these statements are precisely the ones needed to unwrap what the normalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore cochain complex
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+algebra"} cosimplicial algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbf{B} G_e^{(1)})
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence
            is like.
          %h1#definitions_and_setup Definitions and setup
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            (by which we mean a finite dimensional Lie group). Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
            which in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is the cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^k
            with the standard face and degeneray map (see the examples at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            for details).
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            be some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            that models the axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            and which has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
            embedding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hookrightarrow T
          %p
            Consider then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            as a simplicial object in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_e^{(1)} \hookrightarrow G
            be the space that is the first infinitesimal neighbourhood of the neutral element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D(n) = \{(d_1, \cdots, d_n) \in R^n | \forall i,j : d_i d_j = 0\}
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            the dimension of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %span.newWikiWord
              log-exp bijection in synthetic differential geometry
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/log-exp+bijection+in+synthetic+differential+geometry"} ?
            this space is canonically identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi Lie
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g := Lie(G)
            underlying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Moreover, by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axiom"} Kock-Lawvere axiom
            morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : D(n) \simeq G_e^{(1)} \to R
            are necessarily linear
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi d
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : d \mapsto f_0 \to f_1 \cdot d
            = succeed "\u2013" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi log
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} log
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} exp
            bijection are nothing but elements in the dual vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^*
          %p
            Recall that the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
            whose underlying graded-commutative algebra is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmann+algebra"} Grassmann algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo •
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi ⋯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge^\bullet g^* = \mathbb{R} \oplus g^* \oplus g^* \wedge g^* \oplus \cdots
          %p
            So the subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(G_e^{(1)})
            that vanishes at 0 is naturally isomorphic to the degree-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            part of the Chevalley–Eilenberg algebra.
          %p
            Notice now that the multiplication on the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            does not restrict to a multiplication on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_e^{(1)}
            because the sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_1 + d_2
            of two elements that each square to 0 is does in general not square to 0, –
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d_1 + d_2)^2 = 2 d_1 d_2
            but only its cube
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 3
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d_1 + d_2)^3 = 0
            does. Therefore the group multiplication induces a composition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdot :  G_e^{(1)}\times G_2^{(1)} \to G_e^{(2)}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Consider therefore the space of “infinitesimal
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            whose composite is again an infinitesimal
            = succeed "-cell\u201D," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi G
                          %mo ×
                          %mi G
                          %msubsup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi e
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi G
                            %mi e
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %msubsup
                            %mi G
                            %mi e
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo ⋅
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi G
                            %mi e
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↪
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi G
                            %mi e
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 2
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  (G \times G)_e^{(1)}
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  G_e^{(1)} \times G_e^{(1)}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow^{\cdot}
                  \\\
                  G_e^{(1)}
                  \&amp;\hookrightarrow&amp;
                  G_e^{(2)}
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            By item 3) of
            %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/SGM-final.pdf#page=226"} theorem (6.8.1)
            this pullback is precisely the space of elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y) \in G_e^{(1)} \times G_e^{(1)}
            such that not only
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are infinitesimal neighbours of the neutral element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
          %p
            By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axiom"} Kock-Lawvere axiom
            (entirely analogous to the similar step in the derivation of simplicial
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms+in+synthetic+differential+geometry"} differential forms in synthetic differential geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G \times G)_e^{(1)} \to R
            that vanish on degenerate elements are in bijection with antisymmetric maps that are canonically identified with elements in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi ℝ
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^* \wedge_{\mathbb{R}} g^*
            (need to say this in more detail…)
          %p Continuing in this manner (…details for higher degrees to be filled in…) we define the simplicial space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi e
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}G_e^{(1)}
                  \:=
                  (
                  \\cdots (G \times G)_e^{{1}}
                  \\stackrel{\to}{\stackrel{\to}{\to}}
                  G_e^{(1)}
                  \\stackrel{\to}{\to}
                  {*}
                  )
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %em normalized
            Moore cochain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi N
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N^\bullet(C^\infty(\mathbf{B}G_e^{(1)}))
            of the cosimplicial algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi e
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mo ←
                    %mo ←
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ←
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C^\infty(\mathbf{B}G_e^{(1)})
                  \:=
                  (
                  \\cdots C^\infty((G \times G)_e^{{1}})
                  \\stackrel{\leftarrow}{\stackrel{\leftarrow}{\leftarrow}}
                  C^\infty(G_e^{(1)})
                  \\stackrel{\leftarrow}{\leftarrow}
                  {*}
                  )
          %p
            is in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            given by the kernel of the joint degeneracy maps. As in the discussion at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms+in+synthetic+differential+geometry"} differential forms in synthetic differential geometry
            this picks out the functions that vanish on degenerate simplices. So from the above we get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi N
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mn 0
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  N^\bullet(C^\infty(\mathbf{B}G_e^{(1)}))
                  \=
                  (\cdots
                  g^* \wedge g^*
                  \\stackrel{d = \sum_i (-1)^i d_i}{\leftarrow}
                  g^*
                  \\stackrel{0}{\leftarrow}
                  \\mathbb{R}
                  )
                  \\,.
          %p
            Recall that the differential of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            is on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^*
            just the dual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]^* : g^* \to g^* \wedge g^*
            of the Lie bracket
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi g
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi g
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-] : g \otimes g \to g
          %p
            We need to check that this is reproduced by the differential of the Moore cochain complex, which is the alternating sum of the face maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = \sum_i (-1)^i d_i
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in g^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y) \in (G \times G)_e^{(1)}
            that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (d f) (x,y) = f(p_1(x,y)) - f(x \cdot y) + f(p_2(x,y))
                  \= f (x) + f(y) - f(x \cdot y)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Now we use the crucial
            %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/SGM-final.pdf#page=227"} formula (6.8.2)
            from Anders Kock’s book, which says that the group product on the infinitesimal elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \cdot y = x + y + \frac{1}{2}\{x,y\}
                  \\,,
          %p where the last term is the group commutator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{x,y\}:= x \cdot y \cdot x^{-1} \cdot y^{-1}
                  \\,.
          %p
            So this is the term that remains in the formula for
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d f
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (d f)(x,y) = -\frac{1}{2} f(\{x,y\})
                  \\,.
          %p
            On that we apply
            %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/SGM-final.pdf#page=222"} theorem 6.6.1
            of Kock’s book, which says (in its third item) that under the
            = succeed "\u2013" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi log
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} log
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} exp
            bijection by which we identified the infinitesimal neighbourhood
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi G
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_e^{(1)}
            (and functions on it) with the tangent space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_e(G)
            (and linear functions on it) the group commutator maps to the Lie algebra commutator. So indeed under the identification of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            with an element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^*
            we find
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo *
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d f = [-,-]^* f
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is indeed the differential of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
          %p (discussion needs to be completed: situation in higher degree and cup-product mapping to wedge product needs to be discussed…)
